  "interpretation": "D1: on the satellite-product axis -- GPS broadcast SPP, dual-frequency ionosphere-free, and precise (IGS-final satellite clocks) -- the short-baseline correlation agrees to 8%, spanning broadcast vs precise satellite clocks and single vs dual frequency; this rules out satellite-ephemeris and ionospheric artefacts. MGEX multi-GNSS sits -10% from that mean (different constellation set and clock-combination datum), so the all-four spread is 15%. D2: within the dense European aperture (50-1300 km) the correlation is FLAT (far/near = 0.88); this is why 5-station cluster analyses were degenerate -- there is no distance contrast to exploit below ~1300 km. D3: at 152,800 pair-days the EW-NS anisotropy has the sign the series reports (+0.015) but is not significant under station-clustered resampling; the binding constraint is 30 stations in one region, not the number of days."
